WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.219 --> 00:00:20.210
The year is 1955, South Korea is a corrupt
and unstable country, that depends heavily

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:23.130
on foreign aid.

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:31.660
Devastated by the war, South Korea was, at
the time, the third poorest country in Asia.

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:36.570
Its per capita income equaled that of African
countries such as Ghana, it was enduring the

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:41.850
highest inflation and lowest growth rate in
the region.

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:46.870
Ruled with an iron fist by the authoritarian
General Park Chung-Hee from 1961, expectations

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:48.910
for this country weren’t great.

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:53.119
South Korea in the sixties was a country of
emigrants and even its northern neighbor looked

00:00:53.119 --> 00:00:54.230
more promising.

00:00:54.230 --> 00:01:02.640
But, just a second, because that’s not how
things look today.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:09.140
Nowadays, South Korea is the country of K-pop,
K-beauty, K-fashion and K-dramas..

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:17.450
In short, we’re talking about a country
that’s become trendy.

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:27.670
Folks, the Korean Wave, also known as “Hallyu”
has reached virtually every corner of

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:29.570
the globe.

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:36.360
Now, don’t think that it’s only culture
that has been exported, not at all.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:41.520
South Korean companies such as Samsung, Hyundai
or LG have also extended their tentacles across

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:48.049
all five continents.

00:01:48.049 --> 00:02:00.330
And that’s no small feat.

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:04.530
South Korea, has been one of the greatest
economic success stories of all time.

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:08.970
In a little over three decades, that’s just
one generation, South Korea went from being

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:18.310
a third-world country to becoming one of the
richest and most prosperous countries on earth.

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:22.450
So the question is, how did South Korea manage
this development, how did it manage to become

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:23.760
such a rich country?

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.690
Well, actually No., That’s not today’s
question, because we’ve already answered

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:31.760
that in another video, which I’ll link in
the description.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:35.510
In a nutshell it was thanks to the unique
South Korean development model, which is characterized

00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:39.510
by large industrial groups – the “chaebols”
–along with a very demanding educational

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:44.010
system, an open approach to the world, a large
accumulation of capital and active technological

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:49.930
absorption policies, that the so-called South
Korean economic miracle came about.

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:56.750
But, folks… what brought us here today is
that now this “miracle” seems to have

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:58.120
run its course.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:02.569
In 2017 this man won at the elections, the
current president, Moon Jae-in, with a fistful

00:03:02.569 --> 00:03:06.980
of new policies that he promised would turn
South Korea's economic model around.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:11.420
And we aren’t talking about conventional
policies, far from it.

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:14.450
Listen up.

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:16.690
AN EXHAUSTED MODEL

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:23.599
Yes, nowadays South Korea is one of the richest
countries on the planet and... its economy

00:03:23.599 --> 00:03:28.190
continues to grow, but its model is flawed
and many indicators show a negative trend.

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:33.240
For example, it is expected that in 2019 the
country will record the lowest growth of the

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:37.830
last seven years; its unemployment rate hit
a nine-year high in January, wages aren’t

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:40.680
increasing and young people are finding it
harder and harder to get a job.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:45.959
None of this has been very common in South
Korea over the last 30 years.

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:50.879
“Unfortunately, because we ourselves do
not see the future of Korea as so rosy, I

00:03:50.879 --> 00:03:55.050
do not want to bring up children in this unpromising
society.

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:59.599
I think that mentality is persistent among
many of my peers.”

00:03:59.599 --> 00:04:02.750
Bang Seong-deok, 26, a civil engineering doctoral
student.)

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:06.160
This alone should be cause for concern,, right?

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:15.260
Well... wait because there’s more.

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:19.220
Households.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:24.150
South Korean families accumulate debt equivalent
to almost 100% of the Gross Domestic Product,

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:29.440
which makes them the most leveraged households
in any country in the world.

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:31.970
Because, yes…

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:36.372
South Koreans do save a lot, but they also
invest a lot, especially in housing.

00:04:36.372 --> 00:04:41.550
And on top of that, most jobs are so unproductive
that the work day extends well into the evening.

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:44.090
And I’m not talking a couple of hours overtime.

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:57.030
Check this out:
South Korea cuts 'inhumanely long' 68-hour

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:58.160
working week.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:03.090
President introduces 52-hour work week to
help improve quality of life and boost birth

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:04.090
rates.

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:06.940
The Guardian)
If I don’t work overtime, my pay gets reduced.

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:13.220
I’d rather work more to get paid more,”
Lee Myung-ryul, a 37-year-old who earns $1,800

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:15.050
a month at Woongbee TS)

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:18.590
I mean, as you can see, this model is really
unsustainable.

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:20.380
And this is just the tip of the iceberg.

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:24.580
You see, the South Korean economy has some
characteristics that we need to take into

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:28.870
account.

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:34.220
On the one hand, South Korea's economy is
divided, so to speak, into two main parts:

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:36.880
The huge chaebols, which are extremely important.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:42.880
The ten largest groups alone generate more
than 40% of its GDP (44.2% in 2018).

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:47.620
These groups receive favors and privileges
from the government, control financial institutions

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:51.200
and have behaviors that seek to limit competition
within the country.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:55.699
Then there’s the SMEs, small and medium
enterprises.

00:05:55.699 --> 00:06:00.710
More than 3.5 million companies that have
no privileges and are subject to the enormous

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:01.949
power of the chaebols.

00:06:01.949 --> 00:06:06.500
A situation that has very significant consequences.

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:14.410
For example, the average SME salary, which
is what most South Koreans earn, is approximately

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:19.949
half, half that of the large company salaries
and the gap keeps growing.

00:06:19.949 --> 00:06:21.669
Why?

00:06:21.669 --> 00:06:27.050
Well, because among other things, the big
chaebols behave in a monopolistic way and

00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:33.060
there are many suspicions of collusion between
these groups and various politicians.

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:39.889
It goes something like this: the directors
of one large group agree with the directors

00:06:39.889 --> 00:06:44.240
of another group and get some politicians
involved to limit the competition and share

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:45.460
the market between them.

00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:49.380
Faced with this situation, you can already
imagine that the power over SMEs is enormous...

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:54.960
If they don’t accept the chaebol’s conditions
they simply have to shut down.

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:59.370
So this isn’t just a matter of salary differences,
in recent years, the number of SMEs that have

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:06.520
declared bankruptcy has grown exponentially.

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:11.700
As if that weren’t enough, the technological
development of countries like China is increasing

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:19.630
the competition and pressuring the South Korean
economy, which is extremely dependent on exports.

00:07:19.630 --> 00:07:26.820
Well, this is causing the chaebols to transfer
part of their production to other countries

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:29.620
and replace local suppliers with foreign suppliers.

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:35.360
“[Korean companies] can’t compete with
China and India on price and should move up

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:36.530
the value chain.

00:07:36.530 --> 00:07:40.790
But the industrial evolution towards higher
value-added products is not happening in the

00:07:40.790 --> 00:07:44.160
SME sector because of their reliance on the
conglomerates”.

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:48.030
Park Sang-in, an economics professor at Seoul
National University.)

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:58.250
To see an example of this, we need look no
further than the automotive industry.

00:07:58.250 --> 00:08:01.090
South Korea is one of the largest car manufacturers
in the world.

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:05.400
But in spite of the surge that this sector
has experienced in recent years, South Korean

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:08.680
production has been falling for the last three
consecutive years.

00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:09.680
Why?

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:13.050
Because Hyundai, which controls 70% of the
national market, is moving its production

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:18.900
and suppliers to countries such as Mexico
and India.

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:22.200
In the end, folks, the privileges that the
government has given to these groups and the

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:27.539
actions they have taken to limit competition
have meant that most jobs are tied to unproductive

00:08:27.539 --> 00:08:32.510
companies, with little efficiency and little
investment in research and innovation.

00:08:32.510 --> 00:08:37.520
And of course, if you don’t have those things,
then you can’t compete with countries like

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:40.959
China or Vietnam, which have much lower labor
costs.

00:08:40.959 --> 00:08:44.209
And that’s generating a lot of fear in South
Korea.

00:08:44.209 --> 00:08:48.680
But... it was precisely for his promise to
solve these problems that Moon Jae-in, the

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:51.399
current president, won the 2017 elections.

00:08:51.399 --> 00:08:52.679
And, you know what?

00:08:52.679 --> 00:08:55.059
He has an unconventional plan.

00:08:55.059 --> 00:08:58.119
How does Moon Jae-in want to rescue South
Korea?

00:08:58.119 --> 00:09:01.980
Check this out.

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:07.079
(PRESIDENT MOON-JAE IN’S PLAN)

00:09:07.079 --> 00:09:12.199
Dear friends of VisualPolitik, Moon Jae-in
has promised to reform South Korea’s economic

00:09:12.199 --> 00:09:13.939
model from top to bottom.

00:09:13.939 --> 00:09:19.410
And he isn’t planning on doing it with the
typical solutions of lower taxes or less regulation.

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:20.410
Quite the opposite.

00:09:20.410 --> 00:09:24.209
The South Korean president’s plan is based
fundamentally on two pillars.

00:09:24.209 --> 00:09:29.050
The first consists of what its supporters
call “democratizing the economy”...

00:09:29.050 --> 00:09:35.510
… which in practice consists of raising
taxes, increasing public spending, public

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:37.339
employment and the minimum wage.

00:09:37.339 --> 00:09:38.339
The full cocktail.

00:09:38.339 --> 00:09:40.829
And not precisely in small quantities.

00:09:40.829 --> 00:09:48.249
See, in 2019 public spending will grow by
more than 10%, the largest increase in a decade.

00:09:48.249 --> 00:09:53.480
On the other hand, the minimum wage grew by
16.4% in 2018 and in 2019 it will rise by

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:55.220
an additional 10.9%.

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:59.480
And the government has already said that the
raises won’t end there.

00:09:59.480 --> 00:10:04.459
In order to finance more state spending and
the hiring of 850,000 new public employees,

00:10:04.459 --> 00:10:08.600
Moon Jae-in has boosted the corporate profits
tax, which will go from 22 to 25%.

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:14.689
And he’s also going to raise the tax on
the highest wages.

00:10:14.689 --> 00:10:18.449
The idea that the government has is that all
these measures will raise families’ incomes

00:10:18.449 --> 00:10:20.069
and, with it, consumption.

00:10:20.069 --> 00:10:23.360
This would reduce the dependence of South
Korean companies on exports.

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:24.360
The problem?

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:25.360
Well, folks...

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:26.360
It's not working.

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:30.299
See, since Moon Jae-in began taking these
measures, the economy has begun to slow down,

00:10:30.299 --> 00:10:34.439
unemployment has started to grow and to top
it all off, lower family incomes are actually

00:10:34.439 --> 00:10:36.119
dropping.

00:10:36.119 --> 00:10:40.220
Yes, you heard that right.

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:44.009
Their salaries are increasing but many workers
have lost their jobs, so the net result is

00:10:44.009 --> 00:10:48.559
that 20% of families with fewer resources
earn less money today than they did before

00:10:48.559 --> 00:10:51.110
the minimum wage raise.

00:10:51.110 --> 00:10:57.459
All this explains why the president's approval
rating has dropped from 84% in mid-2017 to

00:10:57.459 --> 00:10:58.459
45% today.

00:10:58.459 --> 00:11:04.130
But... folks, before we finish, there’s
another great pillar in the South Korean president’s

00:11:04.130 --> 00:11:05.999
strategy we need to look at.

00:11:05.999 --> 00:11:06.999
Listen up

00:11:06.999 --> 00:11:15.529
(LOOKING NORTH)

00:11:15.529 --> 00:11:24.540
Folks, the South Korean President is convinced
that his country’s future involves North

00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:25.540
Korea.

00:11:25.540 --> 00:11:30.269
"We are at the historical start line toward
common prosperity on the Korean Peninsula

00:11:30.269 --> 00:11:31.809
and Northeast Asia.

00:11:31.809 --> 00:11:34.980
This is an opportunity that has come like
a miracle.

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:38.050
It is a chance that we should never miss.”

00:11:38.050 --> 00:11:39.949
Moon Jae-in)

00:11:39.949 --> 00:11:46.089
Do you see this map?

00:11:46.089 --> 00:11:49.230
In it you can see Moon Jae-in’s plans for
North Korea.

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:53.360
He wants to promote three large economic belts
to connect South Korea’s industrial core

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:58.940
with North Korea, with China and with Russia,
through new rail lines, roads, gas pipelines,

00:11:58.940 --> 00:12:00.809
electrical networks, etc., etc.

00:12:00.809 --> 00:12:05.470
The investments estimated to implement just
the new trains would amount to more than 40

00:12:05.470 --> 00:12:07.449
billion dollars.

00:12:07.449 --> 00:12:12.389
Korea anticipates that the Trans-Siberian
railway will again reach Busan, the southern

00:12:12.389 --> 00:12:17.709
tip of the Korean Peninsula where I grew up,
building on the permanent peace on the Peninsula"

00:12:17.709 --> 00:12:19.620
Moon Jae-in in an address to the Russian Duma.)

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:31.190
Folks, with all these projects, the president
wants to improve the South Korean economy’s

00:12:31.190 --> 00:12:35.379
commercial capabilities, while giving his
companies access to North Korea's resources

00:12:35.379 --> 00:12:36.379
and labor.

00:12:36.379 --> 00:12:39.870
See, it’s estimated that a North Korean
worker’s monthly salary is of around $60,

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:43.819
which is much less than what Chinese or Vietnamese
workers charge.

00:12:43.819 --> 00:12:49.079
"For Samsung Electronics, North Korea is a
good candidate to set up production lines

00:12:49.079 --> 00:12:54.119
because it can offer cheap labor, has no language
barrier and has the same time [zone]," You

00:12:54.119 --> 00:12:57.149
Seung-min, chief strategist at Samsung Securities.)

00:12:57.149 --> 00:12:58.149
The problem?

00:12:58.149 --> 00:13:01.470
Well Moon Jae-in isn’t having much luck
here either.

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:06.329
While, negotiations between North Korea and
the United States are going slowly, South

00:13:06.329 --> 00:13:10.730
Korean companies aren’t convinced about
investing billions of dollars in North Korea

00:13:10.730 --> 00:13:14.309
in the short term, especially because they
don’t trust Kim Jong-Un's regime.

00:13:14.309 --> 00:13:19.949
And then there's the last question, who’s
going to pay the huge price tag required to

00:13:19.949 --> 00:13:21.499
carry out the president's plans?

00:13:21.499 --> 00:13:25.569
To recap, this is the recipe with which Moon
Jae-in wants to transform the South Korean

00:13:25.569 --> 00:13:26.609
economic model:

00:13:26.609 --> 00:13:31.189
More taxes, more public spending, more public
employment, higher minimum wages and development

00:13:31.189 --> 00:13:32.189
in North Korea.

00:13:32.189 --> 00:13:33.579
Do you think it’ll work?

00:13:33.579 --> 00:13:36.980
(Probably Yes / No, they are bad solutions
/ Its easy, socialism never works)

